title: Editing a text file
tags: [[viewer panel]]
kind: reference
aliases: text-editor viewer:edit
summary: The viewer panel edits a text file in place — an edit box, Save greyed while there is nothing to save, Reload to drop the changes — and refuses what it could not write back exactly.

While the [[viewer panel]] shows a text file, its toolbar has an ''edit'' box, and
''Save'' and ''Reload'' buttons. Ticking the box turns the preview into a text field
and gives it the keyboard. The box stays ticked from one file to the next.

|!Key |!Command |!Does |
|<<key "viewer:toggle-edit" "v e">> |[[viewer:toggle-edit]] |ticks or unticks the box |
|<<key "viewer:focus text" "p e">> |[[viewer:focus]] `text` |gives the field the keyboard, ticking the box if needed. `Escape` takes it back, as in any field |
|<<key "viewer:save" "p s">>, and `Ctrl+s` in the field |[[viewer:save]] |writes the changes. Greyed when there are none |
|none |[[viewer:reload]] |reads the file again, dropping the changes |

`Reload` has no key because what it drops cannot be got back ([[Command classes]]).
Elsewhere `Ctrl+s` still saves the workspace.

Unticking the box does not drop anything: the changes stay on screen, read-only, and
can still be saved.

!! Unsaved changes hold the panel

The panel normally follows the selection. While a file has unsaved changes, it stays
on that file, and says so: a move of the selection waits, and the panel goes there
once the changes are saved or reloaded. Going up (<<key "viewer:back" Backspace>>) is
refused the same way, and [[view:refresh]] asks before dropping them.

Closing the workspace or quitting the GUI does not ask: the changes are lost.

!! A change made on disk

Saving first checks the file's size and modification time against those of the last
read or save. If something else wrote to it meanwhile, nothing is written and the
status bar says so. `viewer:save force` overwrites that change; `viewer:reload` takes
it and drops yours.

!! What cannot be edited

Saving replaces the whole file with what the field holds, so a file is editable only
when the field holds it exactly. The box is greyed, and the toolbar says why, for a
file that:

* is larger than the preview (`text-preview-limit`, 256 KiB, in [[GUI configuration]]): the field would hold only its beginning;
* is not UTF-8, UTF-16 for instance: decoding it for display loses bytes;
* holds NUL bytes;
* mixes line endings. A text field only knows LF, so a file with CRLF everywhere is written back with CRLF, but a mix could not be restored.

A byte order mark is kept.

!! How it writes

The content is written in place, not to a temporary file renamed over the original.
The watcher reads a rename onto a tracked path as that file destroyed, and would
orphan its metarecord ([[Event semantics]]). Writing in place also keeps permissions,
hard links and symbolic links. The price is that a crash during the write can leave
the file incomplete.

To the repository, a save is a change like any other program's: the watcher or the
next reconcile records it, and it is not in the [[log|The operation log]], so
`log:undo` does not undo it.
